The patent describes an engravable polymeric gravure printing form produced from a curable multifunctional urethane composition. The formulation generally contains multifunctional urethane oligomers or prepolymers bearing reactive groups, one or more crosslinking monomers, and a thermal or radiation-activated initiator. Optional additives may control viscosity, hardness, adhesion, cure rate, and engraving behavior.

The liquid or moldable composition is applied to a cylindrical printing-form support, cured to form a highly crosslinked polymer layer, and finished to the required surface geometry. Gravure cells are then created by mechanical or laser engraving. The cured urethane network is engineered to provide:

- sufficient hardness and dimensional stability for accurate cell formation;
- controlled toughness and low brittleness to minimize chipping during engraving;
- abrasion, solvent, and ink resistance during printing;
- good adhesion to the underlying sleeve or cylinder; and
- a smooth, polishable surface capable of reproducing fine image detail.

The technology provides a polymeric alternative to conventional metal-plated gravure cylinders, potentially reducing manufacturing complexity, processing time, and environmental burdens associated with metal deposition and etching. The principal patent scope typically covers the reactive urethane composition, the cured engraving layer, methods of forming and curing the printing form, and its use in gravure printing.
